{
  "evidence": "TEXT_F: Budget Responsibility Lock and OBR auditing; immigration measures (1,000 extra border staff, exit checks, two‑year wait for benefits, English requirement, retain non‑EU cap); stronger counter‑extremism (controls on suspects, revamped Prevent, de‑radicalisation, zero‑tolerance of hate crime); defence commitments (NATO, maintain independent nuclear deterrent, SDSR, bolster cyber security); strong Care pledges (8,000 GPs, 20,000 nurses, mental‑health parity with 28‑day access, end 15‑minute care visits, support for carers). TEXT_G: Balance the budget by 2017/18 via taxes on wealth while allowing borrowing for investment; pro‑EU stance; step down nuclear posture (end continuous patrols) and prioritise European cooperation; borders policy with faster asylum decisions and allowing work after six months; civil‑liberties tilt (protect HRA, Digital Bill of Rights, oppose mass surveillance), drug policy shift to health‑led with presumption against short sentences; strong Care pledges (£8bn NHS, mental‑health parity, carers bonus); strong Sanctity/green agenda (Five Green Laws, net zero 2050, MUP for alcohol, tobacco levy).",
  "reasoning": "Both manifestos satisfy high Care with major NHS and mental‑health commitments, but the electorate also places substantial weight on Loyalty, Authority, and Proportionality, where Labour’s extra border staff, two‑year wait for benefits, stronger counter‑extremism, NATO/nuclear stance, and a strict Budget Responsibility Lock with OBR auditing match those priorities. By contrast, the Lib Dems’ pro‑EU stance, reduced nuclear posture, civil‑liberties tilt and more permissive asylum/work rules skew away from those foundations, while their stronger Sanctity/green agenda is least weighted in the profile.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
